A||||10||v8fieroGT||springs?||||1||
Z||000000||v8fieroGT||06-17-2001||05:00 PM||||I want to lower my car 1.5 inches and just wanted to know which springs are not suposse to be cut, front or back ones? I heard that one of them are not suppose to be cut.Does anyone know why? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||MrPBody||06-17-2001||07:29 PM||||Dunno.  I cut all four of mine.  ('85 SE WS6)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||strdream||06-17-2001||07:31 PM||||I think you will find all the info you need here   <A HREF="http://www.iserv.net/~strdream" TARGET=_blank>www.iserv.net/~strdream</A> <BR> <BR>Ross<p>[This message has been edited by strdream (edited 06-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||TRiAD||06-17-2001||09:22 PM||||D'oh!<BR>You don't want to <I>ever</I> cut springs, front or rear. <BR>Springs are made to specific specs, and with inexpensive springs out there that drop the car properly, there's just no reason not to just buy the real ones.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||11
Z||000004||Voytek2||06-17-2001||09:30 PM||||Cut all 4 of mine.  Rides just fine.  Check with Flashy.  His were cut and he got an awesome alignment, too.<BR>This is what you should watch for:<BR>The front ones can be cut no problem.  Measure before you take them out to see how much of a drop you want.  Then measure the springs after removal and take off about the same amount as the desired drop.  It's FAIRLY proportional.  The back's the same way.  If you cut off enough on the back, make sure you adjust the seating of the spring AS you lower the car onto the ground 'cause they may get loose or even fall out of place when cut low. One other thing.  Check the stiffness of your springs.  When springs are cut, they lose some softness.  I believe there were 5 stiffnesses available.  Mine had the stiffest ones.  I ended up buying some softer springs for my '87 GT from an '84.  After cutting them, I have retained about the same ride comfort.  Some people will say to heat them up and compress them.  DON'T do that.<BR>Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000005||v8fieroGT||06-17-2001||09:59 PM||||Thanks  lot guys. I appreciate the help. I'll be cutting them tomorow. I assumed that it was the seat on the springs that had to be the same when you cut them but just wanted to make sure before i went and messed them up.||||reg||1
Z||000006||FLASHY||06-18-2001||02:44 AM||||Nope, you basically can cut them at whatever length you want (within reason of course). It won't affect the seating if your cut is at a different area than before. Once the car's weight is down on the springs, it flattens out the top anyway. Hope this makes sense, it's late and I'm tired. Like Voytek said, I had no problems once I cut my springs. In fact I'd say it improved my ride. It's stiffness has barely changed and I can take corners like you wouldn't believe! Most places told me I couldn't get an alignment because it'll be too low and everything is so screwed up, but that turned out to be FALSE. The guys at Fountain Tire did it with no troubles at all. They did, however, jack up the car at one point and then didn't seat the springs properly when they dropped it back down. The springs had fallen out of the socket and rested on the wheel well. NOT GOOD. But all is fine now.<P>Ok bed time. If something didn't make sense I'll most likely correct myself tomorrow.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/flashy_sig.gif"> <P>"I know you believe you understand what you think I wrote, but I'm not sure you realize that what you read is not what I really meant"||||reg||1
Z||000007||TRiAD||06-18-2001||06:18 AM||||How long have you guys been driving on your cut springs?<P>If/when you're wrong and his springs collapse, are you going to replace the damaged parts for him?<P>Cut springs are just dangerous. You will eventually damage your other suspension components. ||||reg||5
Z||000008||rockcrawl||06-18-2001||07:23 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Cut springs are just dangerous. You will eventually damage your other suspension components.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Please explain how/why they are dangerous and how they will damage other suspension components.||||reg||5
Z||000009||mrossum||06-18-2001||09:40 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>How long have you guys been driving on your cut springs?<P>If/when you're wrong and his springs collapse, are you going to replace the damaged parts for him?<P>Cut springs are just dangerous. You will eventually damage your other suspension components. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>been driving on my cut springs for 5 years, now. even race the car occasionally. cut springs are not going to be dangerous or weak, as long as you cut them properly. the key is not to get the rest of the spring to hot. i cut mine with a chop saw. it kept the heat confined to the cut area only. i wouldn't recommend using a torch. just my 2 cents worth. good luck. i have several sets of spare stock springs if you cut them to short............. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||elfiero||06-18-2001||10:28 AM||||These guys are correct,you can cut springs<BR>I've done it many times.Like the man says,use a<BR>cutoff wheel or chop saw.To understand what<BR>happens when you cut a spring,picture this,<BR>how a spring works is-unwind the entire spring<BR>and anchor one end in a vice.grab a hold of<BR>the other end and twist-lets say you can twist it 4 times before you just cant twist<BR>any more(remember,this is just to show how a<BR>spring works)now,cut off 8" and twist it again,now you will only be able to twist it<BR>3.25 times with the same amount of force.so<BR>when you change the length of a stock spring<BR>all your changing is the rate of the spring.So when you lower your car by cutting the springs,<BR>you actually get higher rate springs,but the<BR>stock springs are pretty soft to begin with<BR>so I don't think it degrades the ride too<BR>much,but it sure makes the handling better!<BR>REMEMBER-shorten the susp.stops in the front<BR>1" or you will "bottom out" this is still in<BR>the range of travel for the stock shocks,so<BR>they will not be damaged.BTW,to lower 1.5",<BR>cut off 1 1/2 rounds from the bottom of the<BR>spring.this info is for the front end-in the<BR>rear I reccomend coilovers!<p>[This message has been edited by elfiero (edited 06-18-2001).]||||reg||6
